you stimulated a bunch and  record the potential it's greater this  is in essence sort of a memory that  happens in this chunk of brain tissue  and what had been shown before by a  number of labs was that if you do this  in the presence of this a beta oolagah  mer this potential is suppressed so they  can't remember the fact they got jolted  here and if you do this entire  experiment this effect of the a beta ala  Goomer in a piece of brain tissue that  genetically doesn't have prior  now there's no effect so the slices are  protected from the bad effect they  require prayin protein in order to have  this buyout effect another thing that  happens later is that synapses are  actually lost so this is zoomed in on  one of these pieces of a neuron and  culture and you can see each of these  little synapses and if you watch them  over several hours you can see that some  of them in the presence of a beta go  away they shrivel up they're lost this  is what we worry happens in the  Alzheimer's brain and so we did this  kind of experiment in neurons that don't  have prion protein and these synapses  aren't lost instead of losing 10 or 15%  of the synapses in this time period  without prion protein the neurons don't  lose their synapses and then we wanted  to see if it was important really for  something that's a little bit more like  dementia can we test memory now there's  not any perfect model of Alzheimer's  disease that we can test in the  laboratory but as mentioned before there  are these cases of Alzheimer's disease  where single genes can cause the effect  in humans and so these have been  transferred into mice and the mice get  plaques this is a piece of brain with a  bunch of those plaques and the mice get  memory loss and this is just a picture  of how we test memory so the mice go  into a big tank of water and somewhere  in the tank of water there's a platform  where they can climb out of the water  and after they've gone in the tanks a  bunch of times they learn that the  platform is here and so a healthy Mouse  when it's putting this thing will go  directly here and swim around to get out  of the water these are the Alzheimer's  mice that have they have these two genes  from human Alzheimer's cases they don't  know where to go they wander all over if  the mice have these Alzheimer's genes  and have plaques but they don't have  this prion protein now their memory is  over here it's it's improved so getting  rid of this prion protein might actually  benefit the ill effects of a beta Allah  Goomer and in fact a study at NYU showed  an antibody to prayin protein that these  Alzheimer's mice were corrected in their  memory and there's a NIH support now at  Yale to look for drugs that will block  this prion protein and achieve this kind  of genetic effect and also the drug  company AstraZeneca is working in that  direction with antibody so I told you  about this a beta oolagah mer binding to  prayin protein in studies that I'm not  going to go into we've actually mapped  out a couple of the molecular steps in  the nerve in the neuron right after this  happens and one of these is after prayin  protein is this molecule called M glue R  5 and a second one is this molecule  called fin and the reason I mentioned  these is being important these second  steps is that there are known compounds  that interact with each of them so this  molecule M tap blocks this blue protein  M glue r 5 and if this is given to these  same Alzheimer's mice it has a  beneficial effect so this is what's  scored here is actually multiple swims  as the animals are learning so wild-type  mice quickly learn where that hidden  platform was that is they go to the  small circle in that tank I showed you  before the Alzheimer's mice when they're  aged they don't do well here so they  learn slowly and additionally to having  bad memory later but if you treat these  Alzheimer's mice with this drug now they  learn just as well as healthy mice do  their time to get to that platform drops  right down and so this is something that  we can hope to to think about studying  in humans and they're actually here at  Yale it's been developed ligands pet  ligands that can image this particular  blue molecule in a human brain using  positron emission tomography and the  third step in this process is this  molecule fin kinase it's also critical  for this whole pathway and in fact here  again if you see the synapse loss in an  in a neuron that doesn't have  and it doesn't happen so this Finn  kinase is actually a member of a 4 or 5  kinase family called the Sartre family  it is a tie to this phospho to protein  and the tangles of Alzheimer's disease  so it really knits this the two major  pathologies of similary tangles and a  beta plaques together but importantly  there's a drug that targets this kinase  it was originally developed for cancer  it doesn't work too well in oncology but  it's safe and there's actually now a  Yale initiated trial to test this in  Alzheimer's disease and this has been  funded again by the National Institutes  of Health so I'm going to conclude here  and make a few points I told you about  these three molecules potential new  targets to approach the Alzheimer's  problem this is certainly there's many  approaches to the problem and I think we  need to pursue multiple lines of  evidence so we can actually get one of  these disease modifying therapies tau  pathology downstream here not only is it  downstream of this pathway once it's  triggered it can actually maintain  itself and spread and this is an active  area of an investigation to try to block  the ongoing tau pathology and as dr.  Hodes mentioned the immune system plays  a big role in regulating the responses  to a beta 2 tau and the entire synaptic  dysfunction that's in the brain it's  also a lot of evidence that Alzheimer's  disease interacts in a sort of multi  disease fashion with both diabetes and  vascular disease those are important  additional places for interaction and  we're also making a lot of progress in  other forms of dementia which teach us  something about Alzheimer's disease the  two major ones being Lewy body dementia  and frontotemporal dementia and in  conclusion I think we just we need to  know a lot more about the molecular  mechanisms so that we can get to a  disease modifying therapy to stop this  this burgeoning this tsunami of  Alzheimer's disease  threatens to overwhelm our healthcare  system and on a personal level is  devastating to those whose intellect is  robbed and I'm just gonna stop there and  mention the people in my lab who did  this work and most of this has been  supported by various organizations in  the NIH Thank You dr. Strittmatter
 

